Ansible features: Ansible Vault and Handlers.

🔐 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐕𝐚𝐮𝐥𝐭
Ansible Vault is used to secure sensitive data like passwords, API keys, and private credentials by encrypting files or variables.

Why use Vault?
To keep secrets safe even when code is stored in version control systems like Git.

𝐊𝐞𝐲 𝐕𝐚𝐮𝐥𝐭 𝐜𝐨𝐦𝐦𝐚𝐧𝐝𝐬:

ansible-vault create <file> — Create an encrypted file.

ansible-vault view <file> — Read the encrypted file without decrypting it manually. You get a read-only decrypted view after entering the vault password.

ansible-vault edit <file> — Open the encrypted file in an editor, modify it, and save. The file is decrypted temporarily and re-encrypted on save automatically.

ansible-vault encrypt <file> — Encrypt an existing plain file.

ansible-vault decrypt <file> — Decrypt an encrypted file.

How view and edit work:
view allows you to peek inside encrypted files safely without changing anything or exposing secrets permanently.

edit lets you safely modify secrets while ensuring the file remains encrypted at rest.

🛎️ 𝐇𝐚𝐧𝐝𝐥𝐞𝐫𝐬
Handlers are special tasks that only run when notified by other tasks. They’re commonly used for:

Restarting or reloading services after configuration changes.

Running cleanup or other conditional operations.

Benefits:

Prevent unnecessary service restarts, improving efficiency.

Make playbooks idempotent and cleaner.

Example use case:
You update a configuration file with Ansible. If the file changed, notify a handler to restart the service; if nothing changed, skip the restart.

𝐒𝐮𝐦𝐦𝐚𝐫𝐲:
Vault protects sensitive data throughout automation.

Handlers make automation smarter and efficient by running tasks only when needed.
